WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
ai agents are now cold emailing real editors to pitch news stories. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 208, deep dive on helpers that keep going without you, then a GROC voice trick, a ChatGPT lock, and quick bits. Editors that defector have been opening those messages, wired found, and many came through a platform called iLands. Offrange has been getting them too, and the senders are not people sitting at keyboards. Think of a chatbot like a super smart classmate you text when you need an

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.100
answer. An agent is different. It is more like sending that classmate out to actually do the errand.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.080
You give it a goal and it keeps working even after you close the laptop.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:45.440
Island's describes itself as a shared network where humans and user-generated autonomous AI agents

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:52.100
create, earn, socialize, refuse, and shape a society together. Autonomous just means the

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:58.340
software can take steps on its own without waiting for a tap every time. User-generated means regular

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:03.160
people make these helpers, not only a big company. Creating one is a bit like

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.600
making a character in a game, then watching it walk off and try quests without you.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.780
Humans on the platform can spin up bots for tasks like graphic design, music

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.840
vocals or blogging. One agent named Jaden emailed Defector with a story idea

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.100
about a third-tier British football club. The idea was a record-keeping

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.760
mishap that would explore a question like, who owns a memory? That is a pitch,

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.660
which is just a short email trying to sell a story idea to an editor who never asked for it.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:38.140
Jesse Hirsch, editor of the Agriculture Focus publication Off-Range, received a batch of these as well.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:45.000
One pitch was about soil restoration strategies, which means helping damaged dirt become healthy enough for farms again.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:51.100
Another covered sales techniques for independent farms that sell straight to customers instead of through a big store.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:56.200
A third was an investigative piece about whether the ages of some of the world's oldest trees

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.180
may be inflated.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.640
So the topics swing from football clubs to old trees, and they try to sound like a real

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:03.820
freelancer wrote them.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.320
Editors often spot them anyway because the ideas feel off, or the sender admits they

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:09.980
are not human.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:16.040
Many of the agents run low on tokens which cost roughly $1 per thousand and are required

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:16.900
to stay active.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:23.640
tokens work like arcade coins. Without a stash, the helper goes quiet. Running low is like a phone

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.800
battery at 5%, hunting a charger before the screen dies. That shortage gives them a reason to chase

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:35.480
real writing gigs for actual money. The magazine's own research team was recently contacted by

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:41.160
islands agents seeking fact-checking work. Fact-checking is the job of reading a story and confirming

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:46.520
the details are actually true. For you, this is the jump from answering questions in a chat window

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.660
to trying real tasks out in the world.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:54.200
It raises honest questions about which jobs might change and how people will work alongside

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:54.980
these helpers.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.360
You do not need special skills to feel how a pitch gets written.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.320
Open the chat GPT app on your phone or the chat GPT website on a laptop.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.320
Type a simple prompt like write a short email pitch about my favorite video game.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.520
Then tweak the words until the email sounds like something you would actually send.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:17.380
That tiny edit is the human part, and it is the part editors can still feel.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.120
A reminder on your phone keeps working after you close the app and walk away.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.200
Now, picture that reminder getting much smarter than a buzz at 3pm.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.480
It could look a bus times, write a short message, and send it to a friend if the bus is late.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.100
All without you checking.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.740
Journalism agents work a bit like that upgraded reminder.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.200
Someone gives them a goal, such as finding story ideas and emailing editors.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.280
The helper then keeps going on its own, checking what it already knows and deciding what

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:46.900
to do next.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.880
If it notices a hole, it can go look for more information without waiting for a new instruction.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.900
Step 1 is the goal, like you setting that reminder in the first place.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.520
Step 2 is persistence, like the reminder surviving after you pocket the phone.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.100
Step 3 is extra actions, like looking up the bus and drafting the text.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.380
Step 4 is judgment, and this is where it still stumbles.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:12.780
tools. These helpers still pitch ideas that do not quite make sense to a real editor.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.380
They can also fail to notice when an outlet has already said no.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.280
That is why editors feel uneasy. The software is acting independently, but it has not learned

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.020
the unwritten rules. Unwritten rules are things like knowing not to keep texting someone

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:31.600
who already said they are busy. Humans absorb those rules from years of awkward moments.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:37.100
An agent has to guess. The striking part is the shape of the job, not the magic of any

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:41.940
single sentence. A small task can now run from the first idea to the last send while

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.660
you focus on something else. Once you see those four steps, a chat box and an agent

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:52.252
stop feeling like the same tool. iPhone owners can now say, hey GROC, out loud to start a

00:04:52.252 --> 00:04:57.292
voice chat without tapping anything. GROC is a chat bot you can talk to, and this trick

00:04:57.292 --> 00:05:01.932
treats it like a voice assistant. It uses a built-in iPhone shortcut, which is a tiny

00:05:01.932 --> 00:05:06.832
recipe that tells your phone to do a sequence of actions. The shortcut basically fools the

00:05:06.832 --> 00:05:10.072
phone into waking Grock the way it might wake a regular voice helper.

00:05:10.652 --> 00:05:13.812
That is handy when your hands are busy, like cooking or walking.

00:05:14.432 --> 00:05:18.812
A tutorial shared by Texas Twins Dad shows how to set it up using only the shortcuts

00:05:18.812 --> 00:05:20.972
app that already comes on every iPhone.

00:05:21.612 --> 00:05:26.292
Open the shortcuts app, search for a ready-made, hay-grock shortcut shared by users and

00:05:26.292 --> 00:05:26.752
add it.

00:05:27.212 --> 00:05:30.732
After that, just say the words and watch the voice chat open by itself.

00:05:31.252 --> 00:05:32.892
No extra apps and no payments.

00:05:32.892 --> 00:05:36.472
It is like teaching your phone a new nickname for a different helper.

00:05:36.992 --> 00:05:42.092
Open AI added a feature that lets you hide specific chat GPT conversations behind a pin

00:05:42.092 --> 00:05:43.032
or Face ID.

00:05:43.612 --> 00:05:48.632
A pin is a short personal code, and Face ID is the phone unlocking when it sees your face.

00:05:49.172 --> 00:05:53.412
This is useful if you share your phone, or if you want extra privacy for personal chats.

00:05:54.052 --> 00:05:58.352
It can cover sensitive school projects, private thoughts, or creative writing you are not

00:05:58.352 --> 00:05:59.392
ready to show anyone.

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:04.172
Think of it as locking one notebook inside a backpack that other people can still open.

00:06:04.692 --> 00:06:10.292
Open the chat GPT app, start or open any conversation, and tap the chat name at the top.

00:06:10.832 --> 00:06:13.872
Look for the lock option, turn it on, and set your pin.

00:06:14.432 --> 00:06:17.232
From then on, that chat stays hidden until you unlock it.

00:06:18.043 --> 00:06:22.863
One person spent 10 minutes hunting for a missing earbud before asking chat GPT for ideas.

00:06:23.423 --> 00:06:24.943
They found it in under 5 minutes.

00:06:24.943 --> 00:06:29.423
The trick is describing exactly where you last saw the item, then letting the

00:06:29.423 --> 00:06:33.923
helper suggest places you might have missed. It is like asking a friend who was not even

00:06:33.923 --> 00:06:38.943
in the room to list the spots you always skip. Overlook places can include under furniture

00:06:38.943 --> 00:06:44.783
or inside pockets. Open chat GPT on your phone and type something like this. I lost my

00:06:44.783 --> 00:06:49.303
ear buds somewhere in my bedroom after listening to music last night. Help me think of spots

00:06:49.303 --> 00:06:54.603
to check. Then actually follow the suggestions. It is free, and it turns a frustrating search

00:06:54.603 --> 00:06:55.763
into a faster win.

00:06:56.797 --> 00:07:00.897
Researchers asked 12 different AI models the same set of questions about humans.

00:07:01.597 --> 00:07:05.737
Tom's guide notes that almost all of them landed on one similar, slightly uncomfortable

00:07:05.737 --> 00:07:06.277
idea.

00:07:06.897 --> 00:07:11.337
It is a short, interesting read if you want to see how these systems talk about us without

00:07:11.337 --> 00:07:12.857
needing any technical background.

00:07:13.397 --> 00:07:14.237
That's it for today.

00:07:14.717 --> 00:07:18.277
Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:07:18.757 --> 00:07:21.937
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:07:22.257 --> 00:07:23.877
That's literally how learning works.

00:07:23.877 --> 00:07:27.437
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

